Chapter: DeskWindowOriginTest
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopComposite
  cites Works chapter GopFont
  cites UI chapter Theme
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites UI chapter BoxModel
  cites UI chapter Event
  cites Kernel chapter BitmapFont

 A window is a tree painted somewhere other than the screen corner, and
 `comp-render` laid out at a hard-coded `0, 0`. `comp-render-at` takes an
 origin, and the three things that have to hold are geometry, containment and
 HIT.

 The hit row is the one worth writing. The origin goes into the layout rect,
 not into the paint, so the bounds that come back are absolute logical
 coordinates and `comp-hit-x` and `comp-hit-y` keep working with no origin of
 their own. That is a claim about a function this chapter does not change, so
 it is asserted rather than assumed: a point inside the offset window must
 find the offset widget, and the SAME point against the same tree laid at the
 corner must not, which is what says the hit path is origin-sensitive at all
 rather than agreeing by luck.

 Containment is the other half a window needs and a scrolling viewport did
 not: what a window paints must stay inside the rectangle it was granted, or
 the pane underneath is corrupted rather than merely overdrawn.

 THAT ROW READ 0 TWICE BEFORE IT MEANT ANYTHING. With a tree that fits its
 window, widening the clip to the whole buffer changes nothing, because
 layout was keeping the paint in and the clip was never consulted. With a
 tree that overflows past the END of the buffer, the widened clip faults
 instead of reporting. Both read as an arm that passes. The geometry below is
 chosen so the overflow lands INSIDE the buffer and OUTSIDE the grant, which
 is the only arrangement where the number can move: measured 2026-08-24, 0
 with the clip and 3,708 pixels without it.

Section: Geometry

 The buffer is tall enough to HOLD the overflow. A window whose overflowing
 children would land past the end of the buffer cannot distinguish a clip
 that works from a clip that is absent: the first sabotage of this arm
 widened the clip and the third child landed outside the allocation, so the
 run faulted instead of reporting, and a narrower widening changed nothing
 because the overflow was past both boxes. The window is 40 device rows at
 y 40 and the third child lands at y 120, inside the buffer and well outside
 the grant.

  wo-w : Integer = 160
  wo-h : Integer = 160
  wo-s : Integer = 2
  wo-ox : Integer = 40
  wo-oy : Integer = 40
  wo-ww : Integer = 80
  wo-wh : Integer = 40

  wo-cells : Integer = 25600
  wo-bytes : Integer = 102400
  wo-sentinel : Integer = #111111

Section: Counting

  wo-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wo-fill (b) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0 else let p = poke-32 b (i * 4) wo-sentinel in wo-fill b (i + 1) n

 Touched pixels OUTSIDE the granted rectangle, walked as x and y rather than
 as a flat index, because the interesting failure is a row running past the
 right edge and the flat walk cannot tell that from the next row starting.

  wo-outside : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wo-outside (b) (x) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= wo-h then acc
    else if x >= wo-w then wo-outside b 0 (y + 1) acc
    else let inside = x >= wo-ox & x < wo-ox + wo-ww & y >= wo-oy & y < wo-oy + wo-wh
    in let touched = if peek-32 b ((y * wo-w + x) * 4) == wo-sentinel then 0 else 1
    in wo-outside b (x + 1) y (acc + (if inside then 0 else touched))

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

  wo-kid : WidgetNode, Integer -> LayoutRect
  wo-kid (n) (i) = (list-at (n.wn-children) i).wn-bounds

  wo-hit-id : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wo-hit-id (root) (mx) (my) =
    when ev-hit-widget root (comp-hit-x mx wo-s) (comp-hit-y my wo-s)
      is Just (n) -> n.wn-id
      is None -> "none"

Section: Subject

  wo-block : Text -> WidgetNode
  wo-block (id) = widget-set-flex (widget-set-min (widget-panel id DirColumn 0 []) 40 20) 0

 THE TREE MUST OVERFLOW THE WINDOW OR THE CONTAINMENT ROW IS VACUOUS. Three
 blocks of 20 logical rows each go into a window 30 logical rows tall, so the
 last two lie below it and only the clip keeps them off the pane underneath.
 With a tree that fits, widening the clip to the whole buffer changes nothing
 and the row reads 0 either way, which is an instrument that cannot fail.

  wo-tree : WidgetNode
  wo-tree = widget-panel "win" DirColumn 0 [wo-block "one", wo-block "two", wo-block "three"]

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let font = cbf-init
    in let th = theme-terminal
    in let buf = __heap-save
    in let adv = __heap-advance (wo-bytes + 256)
    in act
      let f0 = wo-fill buf 0 wo-cells
      in let corner = comp-render-at buf wo-w font gfont-none wo-tree th 0 0 wo-ww wo-wh wo-s
      in let c0 = wo-kid corner 0
      in let f1 = wo-fill buf 0 wo-cells
      in let win = comp-render-at buf wo-w font gfont-none wo-tree th wo-ox wo-oy wo-ww wo-wh wo-s
      in let w0 = wo-kid win 0
      in let w1 = wo-kid win 1
      in let outside = wo-outside buf 0 0 0

      in let probe-x = (w0.lr-x + w0.lr-w / 2) * wo-s
      in let probe-y = (w0.lr-y + w0.lr-h / 2) * wo-s
      in let hit-win = wo-hit-id win probe-x probe-y
      in let hit-corner = wo-hit-id corner probe-x probe-y

      in act
        print-line-uni ("child moved by the origin   : " & yn (w0.lr-x == c0.lr-x + wo-ox / wo-s & w0.lr-y == c0.lr-y + wo-oy / wo-s))
        print-line-uni ("second child follows it     : " & yn (w1.lr-y > w0.lr-y))
        print-line-uni ("window rows, logical        : " & integer-to-text (wo-wh / wo-s))
        print-line-uni ("third child y and h         : " & integer-to-text ((wo-kid win 2).lr-y - (wo-oy / wo-s)) & " " & integer-to-text ((wo-kid win 2).lr-h))
        print-line-uni ("painted outside the grant   : " & integer-to-text outside)
        print-line-uni ("hit the offset child        : " & hit-win)
        print-line-uni ("same device point, corner   : " & hit-corner)
        print-line-uni ("hit is origin-sensitive     : " & yn (hit-win == "one" & hit-win /= hit-corner))
      end
    end
  end

